---
source: tests/cli.rs
info:
  program: ntropy
  args:
    - site
    - "--help"
  env:
    EDITOR: "true"
---
success: true
exit_code: 0
----- stdout -----
Export the vault as a static website.

Every note becomes a page under `notes/`, beside a tag tree, one page per configured view and group, and a front page. Pages carry a sidebar, breadcrumbs, an outline, and previous/next links. The site works when opened straight from disk; no server is needed.

The look comes from `[site] theme` in `.ntropy/config.toml`, a directory in `.ntropy/themes/site/`; `--theme` overrides it for one invocation. `site theme init` copies the built-in theme into the vault.

Usage: ntropy site [OPTIONS] --output <DIR> [QUERY]...
       ntropy site <COMMAND>

Commands:
  theme  Manage site themes
  help   Print this message or the help of the given subcommand(s)

Arguments:
  [QUERY]...
          A query DSL expression restricting the exported notes (joined from trailing arguments; omitted = every note)

Options:
      --vault <PATH>
          Operate on the vault at this path (overrides all other resolution)

  -n, --non-interactive
          Force non-interactive (plain) behavior even on a TTY

  -o, --output <DIR>
          The output directory. A non-empty directory is refused unless `--force` empties it first

      --strict
          Treat malformed/badly-named notes as errors instead of warnings

      --theme <NAME>
          Export with this site theme instead of the vault's configured one.
          
          Names the directory `<vault>/.ntropy/themes/site/<NAME>/`. `default` selects the built-in theme, overriding a configured one.

      --force
          Empty a non-empty output directory before writing

  -i, --identity <PATH>
          Use this age identity file instead of the OS credential store.
          
          `$NTROPY_IDENTITY` is consulted when the flag is absent.

  -p, --print
          Print the path of the site's `index.html` to stdout on success

      --passphrase-file <PATH>
          Read the vault passphrase from the first line of this file.
          
          Supplies the passphrase wherever one would otherwise be typed: creating an encrypted vault, unlocking one, or converting one. On `vault passphrase` it is the *current* passphrase.

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

